    "summary": "Historic Wall of Sound debut with blazing jams and stellar second set",
    "blurb": "The show marks the first night of the Wall of Sound, delivering a fiery Playing In The Band → Uncle John's Band → Morning Dew corridor. Jerry Garcia’s guitar soars on Black Throated Wind and El Paso while Phil Lesh provides massive bass on the jams. Second‑set highlights include Bertha, Wharf Rat and a seamless U.J.B. sandwich.",
    "review": "On March 23, 1974, the Grateful Dead rolled into the Cow Palace with the brand‑new Wall of Sound in full force. The first set is a mixed bag – some listeners note an uneven start as the massive PA system settles – but highlights like \"Black Throated Wind\" and the blistering \"El Paso\" already hint at the firepower to come. The real fireworks erupt in the second set, where a legendary jam corridor of Playing In The Band → Uncle John's Band → Morning Dew unfolds, punctuated by a seamless U.J.B. sandwich that many reviewers call historic. Jerry’s guitar blooms like fireworks on his solos, Phil’s bass is unstoppable and drives the low‑end wall, while Keith’s organ adds melodic depth. Bill Kreutzmann’s kick drum is superbly miked, especially during the China Cat Sunflower / I Know You Rider segment. The encore rounds it out with a rousing \"Casey Jones\" and \"One More Saturday Night,\" leaving fans raving about one of the most energetic shows of ’74.\n\nMultiple recordings capture this night in striking detail – from crystal‑clear audience tapes that preserve every nuance of the Wall to soundboard mixes that showcase the deep low end. While a few sources mention minor mix quirks (a high Bob vocal or occasional flanging on \"Ship Of Fools\"), the consensus is clear: this performance stands as a milestone in Dead history and an essential listen for anyone chasing the magic of the Wall of Sound debut.",
